From e74ba102f7e8d409cef700d2f7f7e7db77b85fbb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 20:57:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): a land-turn owner ranks ahead of aged work at
 the priority cap [daveey]

Problem (t_e1e58febfc0fae2d): state PR #3207 was approved at its exact head with 13/13 checks green from
15:12Z on 2026-11-15, and its owner card t_37acb422ca465f9e held the state land turn until 16:30Z. The
card re-entered ready at 15:12:26Z (condition_met on its CI wake) and was next claimed at 16:36:46Z
(worker-4), 6 min after the turn expired. That run found an overlapping turn and 27 drifted base commits:
another refresh, CI round and delta review (t_ae47e8c79ed22d88). Same pattern at 12:54->13:44->14:59Z and
16:54->20:34Z; four review cards were spent on a patch whose verbatim patch-id never changed.

Why 1: the owner waited 84 min in ready. Hub events 15:46:16Z chat_wait_unclaimed admission
  {reason: llm_pacing, provider openai-codex paced}; dispatch_skipped llm_paced limit 1 running 1.
  Each worker runs one codex card; the next freed slot goes to the best-ranked codex card.
Why 2: the owner was not best-ranked. kanban_admission_score.rank orders by
  min(1000, priority) + 25 * ready_minutes; the owner and the cards ahead of it were at the 1000 cap
  (all 9 of the ready hub cards above 900 at 20:47Z are at 1000), so age decided,
  and a card just back from a park restarts its ready clock (_ready_since: latest condition_met /
  promoted). The body's 16:55Z reading: score 1025, 21st of ~28.
Why 3: the existing turn-holder mechanism could not lift it. The turn-urgency observation
  (kanban_turn_urgency.read_owners) feeds _apply_turn_floor, which sets max(priority, 900): a no-op for
  a card already at 1000 (test_kanban_turn_score asserted the floor value, never the resulting order).
Why 4 (detection): the floor was tested as "effective priority becomes 900", not "the owner is
  dispatched before others"; no test put an owner behind aged cards at the cap. Separately, the
  observer itself has published nothing since ~06:06Z (hub: "gh output too large", owned by
  t_12e8775bde14262e), so even a working lift had no input.

Root (happened): turn-holder urgency was expressed as a priority floor inside a score where priority
  saturates at the cap and age dominates, so holding a turn bought no queue position.
Root (not caught): tests checked the floor's number, not the dispatch order it was meant to produce.
Recurrence: t_ce15faa434176d77 fixed paced cards hiding runnable ones in the pickup window; it did not
  change order among runnable codex cards, so it could not prevent this.

Fix: rank() puts cards the observation names as land-turn/lane owners ahead of every non-owner (owners
keep score order among themselves; score values unchanged; ScoreBreakdown.turn_owner, explain/summary
note it). kanban_board_share.Candidate carries the same tier, so a cross-board host slot also goes to the
owner. A stale, invalid or missing observation names no owners and leaves the order exactly as before.
Entry paths covered, each RED before / GREEN after:
- worker pickup of a hub-homed owner (kanban_worker_node._evaluate): 12 codex cards ready 90 min, owner
  just back from its CI park, codex paced; when one slot frees, pickup(budget=1) offers the owner, not the
  oldest aged card (tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_turn_owner_pickup.py);
- home-gateway dispatch (kanban_db_dispatch.dispatch_once, max_spawn=1): the owner is claimed;
- cross-board shared slot (gateway dispatcher tick, board_share): the owner's board takes the slot;
- rank() with local and replica rows, two owners in score order, explain text.
Not covered: the board-share starvation guard (2 h) still gives a starved board's head the first pick.
